  "page_placements": [
    {
      "kind": "related_finding_example",
      "theme": "Non-basis directions (SAE, sparse coding, PCA, k-means, NMF) are more monosemantic than neurons or basis dimensions",
      "page_url": "https://irregularities.org/polysemantic-neurons/#t-arxiv-2602-22253"
    }
  ],
  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "assumes",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The study measures feature coherence in an AudioLLM using SAE-based and raw-activation methods, a different measurement aimed at the broader polysemanticity question. Its audio setting is not one of the specific claim versions.",
    "gate_note": "The study reports monosemanticity scores for raw activations and SAE features, including comparisons across methods, layers, and expansion factors. These measure feature coherence in an AudioLLM, but do not test the specific systems or mechanisms named in V1–V7. Human naming evaluations and steering results validate the proposed pipeline rather than measuring how common polysemantic neurons are, so they are not recorded as rows.",
    "test_reason": "The paper takes neuron polysemanticity as a premise, citing Toy Models of Superposition, and builds an SAE pipeline to get around it. It never measures how many neurons, or MLP neurons, respond to unrelated inputs. Its only raw-activation numbers are the highest monosemanticity scores of residual-stream dimensions (the 'Poly. Feats.' baseline). These serve as a comparator for the authors' own method. The study reports its pipeline's performance rather than testing any version, so it is T4 and not T2.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is mostly right. The authors conclude that SAE-based AR&D yields interpretable, nameable and steerable audio concepts, beating raw activations and scoring variants. They take neuron polysemanticity as a motivating premise, citing prior work, rather than establishing it.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Uses TopK sparse autoencoders, audio representative-clip ranking, and caption-based concept naming; it does not reproduce the original vision-neuron investigation or its measurement.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Reported monosemanticity varies with layer and expansion factor; deeper layers score higher, while increasing expansion beyond 8 yields only minor gains.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "In Qwen2-Audio-7B-Instruct, the most coherent raw residual-stream dimensions score far lower on the CLAP-based monosemanticity measure (0.68, 1.02 and 1.14 at layers 5, 16 and 26) than the most coherent TopK-SAE features (6.86–9.33). Both kinds score higher in deeper layers.",
        "proposed_claim": "In an audio-language model, raw residual-stream dimensions are much less semantically coherent than SAE features under a top-vs-bottom clip coherence score.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r8",
          "r9",
          "r10",
          "r11",
          "r12",
          "r13",
          "r14",
          "r15",
          "r16",
